Pad data buffer: pressure bytes 16..19 are L1, R1, L2, R2 (the DualShock 2 order)

What it fixes: fillPadStatus (ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp) wrote the pressure bytes of
the 32-byte pad buffer as byte 16 = L1, 17 = L2, 18 = R1, 19 = R2. On a DualShock 2 they are L1, R1,
L2, R2. A game that reads the shoulder buttons through the pressure bytes (after scePadEnterPressMode)
got R1 and L2 swapped, whether the pressure came from the buttons (0 or 255) or from a host source.
Now byte 17 follows R1 and byte 18 follows L2 (the buttons mask and the derived 0/255 value of each).
This is the only place that fills bytes 16..19: the raylib path only produces the button word and
sticks, and the buffer is built by this function; the host source's pressure[] is copied index for index
(pressure[i] -> byte 8+i), so only its documented meaning changes: pressure[8..11] are L1, R1, L2, R2
(runtime/ps2_host_pad.h comment; the struct is unchanged).

How it was verified: tests/runtime/host_pad_source_test.cpp now checks the order explicitly: a source
state with pressure[8..11] = 0x11, 0x22, 0x33, 0x44 gives bytes 16..19 = L1, R1, L2, R2 in that order, and
each shoulder button pressed alone without source pressure sets only its own byte. Against the tree
without this patch the test fails ("R1 pressed alone sets only byte 17", "L2 pressed alone sets only
byte 18"); with the patch it passes, and the other tests of tests/runtime (host_gs_hook, host_loop,
host_vu1_hook, iop_host_spu2, module_buffer_load) pass, built with PS2X_WITH_RAYLIB=OFF, DISPLAY unset.
Other references: PCSX2's DualShock 2 (third_party/pcsx2/pcsx2/SIO/Pad/PadDualshock2.cpp, after Square
come PAD_L1, PAD_R1, PAD_L2, PAD_R2; GPL-3.0).

Origin: the finding and the fix of the byte order come from KillzoneRecomp's patch
patches/ps2recomp/0026-pad-pressure-order.patch (https://github.com/drakolordx7/KillzoneRecomp, commit 5f0700079f9ac4e14799d66d01747dbcdcec55f6 of the local reference copy of that
repository, GPL-3.0). Their patch swaps the two writes and adds a comment; this one does the same and
also ties the indexes 9 and 10 of the host source's pressure[] to R1 and L2 (the byte of each stays 8+i)
and fixes the documentation of runtime/ps2_host_pad.h, which their runtime did not have.

diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_host_pad.h b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_host_pad.h
index 02cf2f0..0c59217 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_host_pad.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_host_pad.h
@@ -23,7 +23,8 @@
 #define PS2_HOST_PAD_MAX_SLOTS 4
 
 // Order of the pressure bytes: the order of the pad data buffer bytes 8..19, i.e.
-// Right, Left, Up, Down, Triangle, Circle, Cross, Square, L1, L2, R1, R2.
+// Right, Left, Up, Down, Triangle, Circle, Cross, Square, L1, R1, L2, R2 (the DualShock 2 order: bytes
+// 16..19 are L1, R1, L2, R2), so pressure[8] is L1, [9] R1, [10] L2, [11] R2.
 #define PS2_HOST_PAD_PRESSURE_COUNT 12
 
 struct Ps2HostPadState
diff --git a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp
index 2571b3a..4ff3c6e 100644
--- a/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/src/lib/Kernel/Stubs/Pad.cpp
@@ -308,9 +308,10 @@ namespace ps2_stubs
             data[13] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnCircle, 5);
             data[14] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnCross, 6);
             data[15] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnSquare, 7);
+            // DualShock 2 order of bytes 16..19: L1, R1, L2, R2 (pressure[8..11] of the host source follow it).
             data[16] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnL1, 8);
-            data[17] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnL2, 9);
-            data[18] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnR1, 10);
+            data[17] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnR1, 9);
+            data[18] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnL2, 10);
             data[19] = pressureValue(state, portState, kPadBtnR2, 11);
         }
 
